  <static seed="112" source="csafranski/2.4Preview_prob1.pg">
    <statement><p>Consider the function <m>f(x) = \tan(x)</m>, and remember that <m>\tan(x) = \displaystyle{\frac{\sin(x)}{\cos(x)}}</m>.</p>
<p>(a) What is the domain of <m>f</m>?</p>
<p><ol form="buttons" marker="A" name="AnSwEr0001">
<li>
<p>All real numbers except zero</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>All real numbers except <m>\pm \frac{\pi}{2}, \pm \frac{3\pi}{2}, \pm \frac{5\pi}{2}, \ldots</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>All real numbers except <m>0, \pm\pi, \pm 2\pi, \pm 3\pi, \ldots</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>All real numbers</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>All positive real numbers.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>None of these</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
<p>(b) Compute <m>f'(x)</m> using the quotient rule. Which of the following is a possible correct answer?</p>
<p><ol form="buttons" marker="A" name="AnSwEr0002">
<li>
<p><m> \displaystyle{f'(x)=\frac{\cos(x)\sin(x)+\sin(x)\cos(x)}{\cos^2(x)}} </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> \displaystyle{f'(x)=\frac{\cos(x)\cos(x)-\sin(x)\sin(x)}{\cos^2(x)}} </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> \displaystyle{f'(x)=\frac{\sin(x)\sin(x)-\cos(x)\cos(x)}{\cos^2(x)}} </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> \displaystyle{f'(x)=\frac{\cos(x)\sin(x)-\sin(x)\cos(x)}{\cos^2(x)}} </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> \displaystyle{f'(x)=\frac{\cos(x)\cos(x)+\sin(x)\sin(x)}{\cos^2(x)}} </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>None of these</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
<p>(c) Simplify the correct answer to part (b) using the Fundamental Trigonometric Identity. Which of the following is a correct answer?</p>
<p><ol form="buttons" marker="A" name="AnSwEr0003">
<li>
<p><m> \displaystyle{f'(x)=\frac{\cos(2x)\sin(2x)}{\cos^2(x)}} </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> \displaystyle{f'(x)=\frac{\cos(x)-\sin(x)}{\cos^2(x)}} </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> \displaystyle{f'(x)=\frac{\sin(x)-\cos(x)}{\cos^2(x)}} </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> \displaystyle{f'(x)=\frac{1}{\cos^2(x)}} </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> \displaystyle{f'(x)=\frac{2\cos(x)\sin(x)}{\cos^2(x)}} </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>None of these</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
<p>(d) Simplify the correct answer to part (c) using another trigonometric identity. Which of the following is a correct answer?</p>
<p><ol form="buttons" marker="A" name="AnSwEr0004">
<li>
<p><m> f'(x) = \csc^2(x) </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> f'(x) = \csc(x) </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> f'(x) = \sec^2(x) </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> f'(x) = \cot^2(x) </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> f'(x) = \sec(x) </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m> f'(x) = \cot(x) </m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>None of these</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
<p>(e) For what values of <m>x</m> is <m>f'(x)</m> defined?</p>
<p><ol form="buttons" marker="A" name="AnSwEr0005">
<li>
<p>All real numbers except zero</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>All real numbers except <m>\pm \frac{\pi}{2}, \pm \frac{3\pi}{2}, \pm \frac{5\pi}{2}, \ldots</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>All real numbers except <m>0, \pm\pi, \pm 2\pi, \pm 3\pi, \ldots</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p>All real numbers</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>All positive real numbers.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>None of these</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{B}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{E}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{D}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{C}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\text{B}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>The tangent function defined everywhere except where the cosine function is zero, 
so its domain is all real numbers except <m>\pm \frac{\pi}{2}, \pm \frac{3\pi}{2}, \pm \frac{5\pi}{2}, \ldots</m>. Using the quotient rule,
<me>\begin{align*}
f'(x) \amp = \frac{\cos(x)\cos(x)+\sin(x)\sin(x)}{\cos^2(x)} \\
\amp = \frac{1}{\cos^2(x)} \\
\amp = \sec^2(x).
\end{align*}</me>
The derivative has the same domain as <m>f</m>.</p>
</solution>
